As a second example, we consider the ring of polynomial functions
$R=\mathbb{Q}[x_{i,j}]$ on the space of 2 x 4 generic matrices. We set the
complex F to be the map $R^4\to R^2$ given by the generic matrix $(x_{i,j})$.
We compute the third symmetric power G of F, in which case lambda is the
partition $(3)$. By Weyman "Cohomology of Vector Bundles and Syzygies",
Exercise 6.34(d), the Schur complex G is exact except in degree zero. We verify
this by computing the Hilbert series of each homology module of G.



